---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Retrogrades', href: '/explore/retrogrades/' },
];
const motionStages = [
  ['Direct', 'The planet appears to move forward through the zodiac.'],
  ['Slowing', 'Its apparent daily motion decreases as the viewing geometry changes.'],
  ['Station', 'It appears nearly still before the apparent direction changes.'],
  ['Retrograde', 'From Earth, it appears to move backward against the zodiac.'],
  ['Station', 'The apparent backward motion slows to another standstill.'],
  ['Direct', 'Forward apparent motion resumes.'],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Retrogrades Explained"
  description="Retrograde motion is an apparent reversal caused by orbital perspective. Learn how astrologers interpret Mercury, Venus, Mars, and outer-planet retrogrades without treating them as disasters."
  path="/explore/retrogrades/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Retrogrades, explained</h1>
        <p class="lede">Retrograde motion is one of those places where the astronomy is simple and the mythology gets loud. Nothing in space suddenly reverses course. What changes is our perspective, and astrologers have long treated that change in apparent motion as meaningful.</p>
      </div>
      <img class="hero__img" src={ocean.glassWave.src} srcset={ocean.glassWave.srcset} sizes="100vw" alt={ocean.glassWave.alt} width={ocean.glassWave.width} height={ocean.glassWave.height} fetchpriority="high" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>First, see the motion</h2>
      <ol class="motion-sequence" aria-label="Apparent retrograde motion sequence">
        {motionStages.map(([label, note], index) => (
          <li>
            <span class="motion-sequence__number">{index + 1}</span>
            <div><strong>{label}</strong><span>{note}</span></div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <div class="retrograde-layers">
        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">Astronomy</p>
          <h3>An apparent change in direction</h3>
          <p>Earth and the other planet continue in their orbits. The backward-looking path comes from our changing viewing angle.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">Astrology</p>
          <h3>A change of emphasis, not a command</h3>
          <p>Astrologers may associate the period with review, return, reconsideration, revision, or a less straightforward expression of the planet involved.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h2>The astronomy</h2>
      <p>
        Retrograde motion is an effect of perspective. Earth and the other planets orbit the Sun at
        different speeds, so as our viewing angle changes, a planet can appear to slow, stop, and
        move backward against the background stars before resuming its usual direction. The planet
        itself has not reversed its orbit.
      </p>
      <p>
        That apparent path is still observable and predictable. It was tracked long before the
        geometry behind it was understood, which is why retrograde periods became part of
        astrological practice.
      </p>

      <h2>What astrologers make of it</h2>
      <p>
        Retrograde symbolism is often associated with review, return, reconsideration, revision, or
        a less straightforward expression of the planet involved. I prefer to think of that as a
        change of emphasis rather than a command. A retrograde does not tell you to stop living.
      </p>
      <p>
        Contracts still get signed. People still travel, begin relationships, change jobs, and make
        decisions. Sometimes a retrograde period coincides with something being revisited; sometimes
        it does not. The rest of the chart, the exact transit, and the life being lived all matter.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.emptyChair} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The ones people tend to notice</h2>
      <div class="retrograde-cards">
        <article>
          <h3>Mercury</h3>
          <p class="card__meta">Roughly three times a year · about three weeks each</p>
          <p>Communication, information, movement, agreements, and everyday logistics. I pay more attention to what is being reconsidered, clarified, corrected, or brought back for another look.</p>
          <a href="/explore/planets/mercury/">Mercury in the chart</a>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Venus</h3>
          <p class="card__meta">About every 18 months · roughly six weeks</p>
          <p>Relationship, value, taste, money, and desire can come back under examination. The useful question is what you are seeing differently about what or whom you value.</p>
          <a href="/explore/planets/venus/">Venus in the chart</a>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Mars</h3>
          <p class="card__meta">About every two years · roughly two to three months</p>
          <p>Action, desire, conflict, and initiative can feel less straightforward. Effort may need redirection, frustration may need naming, or strategy may need adjustment.</p>
          <a href="/explore/planets/mars/">Mars in the chart</a>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h3>The outer planets</h3>
      <p>
        Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto spend substantial portions of every year
        retrograde. Because that is so routine, I would not treat the mere fact that one is
        retrograde as a dramatic personal event. The question is whether it is making a meaningful
        contact in your own chart and how that contact fits the larger pattern.
      </p>

      <h2>A note on tone</h2>
      <p>
        Retrogrades have become one of astrology's easiest villains. I think that flattens an
        interesting cycle into an excuse. A retrograde can be worth noticing without becoming a
        reason to fear a date on the calendar. Curiosity is more useful here than dread.
      </p>
      <p>
        See <a href="/current-sky/">what's retrograde right now</a>, or read
        <a href="/explore/transits/">how transits work</a> generally.
      </p>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Want to see retrograde cycles in context?"
    body="Current Sky shows the shared cycle as it unfolds. If the question has become personal, the Reading Finder can account for whether you are new to Mo or already working from a natal foundation."
    buttonLabel="See Current Sky"
    buttonHref="/current-sky/"
    secondaryLabel="Find the right reading"
    secondaryHref="/reading-finder/"
  />

  <style>
    .motion-sequence { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-3); padding: 0; list-style: none; }
    .motion-sequence li { display: flex; gap: var(--space-3); align-items: flex-start; padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid var(--border-color, currentColor); border-radius: var(--radius-lg, 1rem); }
    .motion-sequence__number { display: grid; width: 2rem; height: 2rem; flex: 0 0 2rem; place-items: center; border: 1px solid currentColor; border-radius: 999px; font-weight: 700; }
    .motion-sequence li div { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-1); }
    .motion-sequence li span:last-child { font-size: var(--text-sm); }
    .retrograde-layers, .retrograde-cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-4); margin-block: var(--space-6); }
    .retrograde-layers article, .retrograde-cards article { padding: var(--space-5); border: 1px solid var(--border-color, currentColor); border-radius: var(--radius-lg, 1rem); }
    .retrograde-layers h3, .retrograde-layers p:last-child, .retrograde-cards p:last-of-type { margin-bottom: 0; }
    @media (min-width: 768px) {
      .motion-sequence { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
      .retrograde-layers { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
      .retrograde-cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
